      "text": "We want to find all integers $a$ such that the polynomial $x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7$ has an integer root.\nSuppose $r$ is an integer root of the polynomial.  Then by the Factor Theorem, $r$ divides both the constant term (7) and the coefficient of the $x$ term ($a$), so $a$ must be of the form $r \\cdot k$, where $k$ is either 1 or 7.\n\nThere are two remaining cases:\n\nIf $r = -7$, then $a = (-7) \\cdot k$, so $a$ must be of the form $-7k$, where $k$ is a nonzero integer.\nIf $r = 1$, then $a = 1 \\cdot k$, so $a$ must be a positive integer.\n\nBy the Chinese Remainder Theorem, any integer $a = ak + rk$ where $kr$ satisfies $3k(r+1) \\equiv 7 \\pmod{2}$ and $ak \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}$ must be an integer multiple of either $k$ (if $r$ is even) or $7k$ (if $r$ is odd). Combining these facts, we have $a \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}$ or $a \\equiv 7 \\pmod{9}$. Therefore, the solution is $\\boxed{-70, -14, 0, 7}$. The answer is: -70,-14,0,7",
