    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "From the Factor Theorem,\n\\[f(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = (x - r)(x^2 + px + q)\\]\nfor some integers $r,p,$ and $q.$ If $r$ is an integer root of the equation, then $-r$ is also an integer root, and substituting $-r$ into the equation shows that $r^2 + p(2r) + q(3r) + 7 = 0.$\n\nThis means that the sum of the coefficients of the three-degree terms and the constant term is a multiple of $r.$ For $r^2$ to divide the number 7, $r = \\boxed{7\\text{ or }-7}.$\n\nThese are the only integer values of $r$ for which the problem’s full range of possibilities with $-r$ as an additional root is exhausted. Using each value of $r$ manually verifies that exactly two values of $a$ give a polynomial which produces integer values at multiples of $r.$",
