      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Given the polynomial \\( P(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 \\), we need to find all integer values of \\( a \\) such that the polynomial has at least one integer root.\n\nLet's assume \\( r \\) is an integer root of the polynomial. By the factor theorem, we can write:\n\\[\nP(x) = (x - r)(x^2 + bx + c)\n\\]\nExpanding the right-hand side, we get:\n\\[\nx^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = x^3 + (b - r)x^2 + (c - br)x - cr\n\\]\nBy comparing coefficients with the original polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 \\), we have:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nb - r = 3 \\\\\nc - br = a \\\\\n-r = 7\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFrom the third equation:\n\\[\nr = -7\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( r = -7 \\) back into the first and second equations:\n\\[\nb - (-7) = 3 \\implies b + 7 = 3 \\implies b = -4\n\\]\n\\[\nc - b(-7) = a \\implies c + 28 = a\n\\]\n\nSince \\( c \\) must be an integer, we substitute \\( c = 28 \\):\n\\[\na = 28 + 28 = 56\n\\]\n\nThus, the only possible value of \\( a \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{56}\n\\]",
